Experiment: Routing at Network Layer Using Cisco Packet Tracer

Aim
To configure and simulate Static Routing and Routing Information Protocol (RIP) using Cisco Packet Tracer and verify communication between different networks.

Requirements
- Cisco Packet Tracer
- 2 routers
- 2 switches
- 4 PCs
- Copper straight-through Ethernet cables

Part A: Static Routing Protocol Configuration
Algorithm / Procedure
1. Open Cisco Packet Tracer.
2. Place two routers (R1 and R2), two switches, and four PCs.
3. Connect two PCs to each switch and connect each switch to its respective router.
4. Connect R1 and R2 using a serial or Ethernet link.
5. Assign IP addresses to PCs and router interfaces.
6. Configure the router interfaces and enable them using no shutdown.
7. Configure static routes using the ip route command on both routers.
8. Test connectivity using the ping command.
9. Use Simulation mode to observe packet transmission between networks.
Network Topology
LAN 1

192.168.1.0/24

PC0, PC1

Router R1

192.168.1.1

10.0.0.1


Router R2

10.0.0.2

192.168.2.1



Connect R1 and R2 directly using an Ethernet or serial link.
LAN 2

192.168.2.0/24

PC2, PC3


IP Address Configuration
Device	Interface	IP address	Subnet mask
R1	G0/0	192.168.1.1	255.255.255.0
R1	G0/1	10.0.0.1	255.255.255.252
R2	G0/0	192.168.2.1	255.255.255.0
R2	G0/1	10.0.0.2	255.255.255.252
PC0	NIC	192.168.1.2	255.255.255.0
PC1	NIC	192.168.1.3	255.255.255.0
PC2	NIC	192.168.2.2	255.255.255.0
PC3	NIC	192.168.2.3	255.255.255.0
Default gateways:
- PC0 and PC1: 192.168.1.1
- PC2 and PC3: 192.168.2.1
Router Configuration
Router R1
enable
configure terminal

interface gigabitEthernet 0/0
ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit

interface gigabitEthernet 0/1
ip address 10.0.0.1 255.255.255.252
no shutdown
exit

ip route 192.168.2.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.2

end
show ip route


Router R2
enable
configure terminal

interface gigabitEthernet 0/0
ip address 192.168.2.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit

interface gigabitEthernet 0/1
ip address 10.0.0.2 255.255.255.252
no shutdown
exit

ip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 10.0.0.1

end
show ip route


If your router has different interface names, use the interfaces available on your selected model.
Test and Expected Output
From PC0, open Desktop → Command Prompt and enter:
ping 192.168.2.2


Sample output:
Reply from 192.168.2.2: bytes=32
Reply from 192.168.2.2: bytes=32

Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 4, Lost = 0


Observation: Static routes manually specify the next hop to reach a remote network. Use show ip route to verify the static routes marked with S.

Part B: RIP Routing Protocol Configuration
Aim
To configure RIP version 2 on routers in Cisco Packet Tracer and verify automatic route learning between different networks.
Algorithm / Procedure
1. Create the same topology with two routers, two switches, and four PCs.
2. Assign the IP addresses shown in Part A.
3. Enable and configure the router interfaces.
4. Configure RIP version 2 on both routers.
5. Disable automatic classful summarization using no auto-summary.
6. Advertise the directly connected networks using the network commands.
7. Verify the routing table using show ip route.
8. Ping a PC on the remote network.
9. Observe packet transmission in Simulation mode.
Router Configuration
Router R1
enable
configure terminal

router rip
version 2
no auto-summary
network 192.168.1.0
network 10.0.0.0
exit

end
show ip route


Router R2
enable
configure terminal

router rip
version 2
no auto-summary
network 192.168.2.0
network 10.0.0.0
exit

end
show ip route


The interface IP addresses and PC settings must be configured as in Part A. Ensure that the router-to-router link is up before testing RIP.
Test and Expected Output
On R1, enter:
show ip route


Illustrative routing-table entry:
R    192.168.2.0/24 [120/1] via 10.0.0.2


On R2, the corresponding route should appear:
R    192.168.1.0/24 [120/1] via 10.0.0.1


Here, R indicates a route learned through RIP, 120 is the default RIP administrative distance, and 1 is the hop count in this example.

Test connectivity from PC0:
ping 192.168.2.2


Expected result: successful replies from PC2 after routing converges and ARP resolution completes.
Observation Table
